and the worst thing that we can do today  in this recession is to limit the  potential energy in our businesses by  over taxing them over regulating them  prohibiting entrepreneurs from starting  new businesses and unfortunately our  government does that at the state  federal and local levels all the time  I'm Tim Sandefur I'm a principal  attorney at the Pacific Legal Foundation  headquartered in California and I'm the  author of the right to earn a living  economic freedom in the law my book  focuses on the history of the right to  make one's own economic choices the  right to economic freedom which was  protected by English and American courts  as far back as the 17th century and was  well understood and highly regarded by  the founding fathers and is a central  part of the Liberty that the  Constitution protects you know a lot of  time you hear people talk about the  economic freedom as an unenumerated  right well it's not an unenumerated  right Liberty is an enumerated right in  the Constitution and Liberty includes  the right to make one's own choices  about a variety of things including the  right to make economic choices what to  buy and sell what to earn what jobs to  take or not take and that sort of thing  what happened during the New Deal was a  the climax of a gradual transformation  of the law often we talk about the New  Deal as being a sudden change in  American law but what it really was was  the constitutional is a ssin of the  philosophy of progressivism of the  progressive interpretation of the  Constitution that began around the end  of the 19th century the beginning of the  20th century and it began with people  like all the Lindo homes or louis  brandeis who were at first were writing  dissenting opinions right holmes  famously dissented in the case of  Lochner versus New York Brandeis wrote  all these famous dissenting opinions in  1934 what happened is that their ideas  became American law and there were four  big progressive ideas the first one was  that was that rights are just  permissions rights aren't you don't you  aren't entitled to rights because your  human rights are something society gives  you for its own purposes the second idea  was that government exists to make  society nice  the founders thought that government  exists only to protect individual rights  but the progressives came up with all  sorts of new ideas for how to make  society nice and eugenics prohibition  segregation income tags all of these  wonderful new ideas for how to control  society and make it better  zoning we talked about eminent domain  for instance it was the progressives who  first used the word blight to describe  neighborhoods began blight originally is  the determinant for a plant disease  right  but the progressives used it to talk  about neighborhoods because they imagine  society as a plant and government as the  gardener that goes around and prunes the  garden and makes it nice  the third great progressive idea was  judicial restraint that when government  decides to violate your rights  courts should do nothing about it and  then the fourth great progressive idea  was the living Constitution so those  four progressive ideas became American  law in 1934 in a case called nebula in  the versus New York and in nebia what  the court did was it invented the so  called rational basis test rational  basis means government does whatever it  wants and gets away with it as long as  some politician thought it was a good  idea  the thing is constitutional as the  rational basis test and since 1934 to  the present day that's the test that's  applied when it comes to economic  freedom or private property rights now  when you're talking about rights that  the Left cares about things like freedom  of speech you tend to get a higher level  of protection from courts but when we're  talking about economic liberty and  private property rights courts simply  look the other way and in the vast  majority of cases there are exceptions  of course but because of the rational  basis test which is nowhere to be found  in the Constitution because of that  people's economic freedoms in this  country are at risk for some legislators  that can do whatever they want  I'd love time on Nebbia because it's  such an incredibly stupid decision what  happens is 1934 the the depths of the  Great Depression unemployment is like 25  percent or something and the state of  New York of course knows how to solve  this problem obviously the solution is  right before our eyes and that is you  have to make food more expensive right  so that's what they do they pass a law  saying you're not allowed to charge less  than I believe it's 8 cents a quart  milk and Leo never Rochester grocery  store owner sales notes is brought up on  charges for it and the US Supreme Court  says yeah you can you can punish him for  that because if the government wants to  regulate the economy it can do virtually  anything it wants to so the rational  basis test that was basically concocted  out of thin air by the progressive  movement and gradually but applied to  American law with no constitutional  basis that's why you have cases like  kilo or these these law licensing  restrictions that prohibit people from  earning of an honest living what are the  primary abuses of economic liberties I  would say the most obvious one is  antitrust law which is a murky swamp of  precedent that nobody can interpret or  understand that where the same behavior  can be characterized as a  pro-competitive good thing for the  consumer or as an anti-competitive crime  against the people depending entirely on  spin and on how broadly you draw the  circle an example that I give in the  book about this is when the Nestle  corporation tried to buy Dreyer's ice  cream now for Nestle to buy dryers is  obviously not a monopoly in the ice  cream business there's plenty of ice  cream manufacturers and there's no  problem so what the Federal Trade  Commission did was it simply created a  new category of ice cream that it called  super premium ice cream which I guess  means really good ice cream in which  case there's only dryers and Breyers and  now it's a monopoly for Nestle to buy  dryers you see so it depends entirely on  how broadly you decide to characterize  the market you can create a defense  against the laws of the United States  I'm currently representing a moving  company in Missouri in a lawsuit where  the licensing law in Missouri says if  you want to start a moving company you  first have to get permission from all  the existing moving companies what  happens is you apply for a permit and  the government notifies all the existing  moving companies and gives them the  opportunity to object and if they object  you have to go to a board of bureaucrats  and prove to them that there ought to be  a new moving company how do you prove  that there ought to be a new moving  company before Starbucks went into  business how could they have proved to  anybody that we need a new coffee shop  wouldn't you have said there's plenty of  coffee shops out there so it's  impossible  prove this what it does is it gives  existing businesses the opportunity to  prevent fair competition from  entrepreneurs in a business that is a  prime example of the kind of entry-level  business that immigrants and the poor  tend to gravitate to and this is a point  that unfortunately a lot of liberals  tend to ignore which is that the people  who need constitutional protection for  economic Liberty the most are the poor  and members of minority groups it's not  rich white guys who are driving taxi  cabs unfortunately just about all the  Supreme Court justices are hostile to  the idea of economic Liberty with the  possible exception of Justice Clarence  Thomas but they even just as Thomas  treats the idea of protecting rights  under the Due Process Clause with  unwarranted skepticism and as a result  even even he is questionable in the  question of economic freedom on during  the oral argument in McDonald vs Chicago  one of the justices asked well if we  restore the privileges or immunities  clause why wouldn't that mean we'd be  protecting economic liberties as though  this is a damming question to which the  answer is yes of course and the  Constitution does protect economic  liberty economic Liberty was the first  thing mentioned in the 1866 Civil Rights  Act which was passed before the 14th  amendment by the same Congress and for  the same reasons and unfortunately the  justices you know they know very well  that the New Deal precedents that  abandoned protections for economic  Liberty are central to the regulatory  welfare state that we have today and  there they are their primary loyalty is  to preserving the regulatory welfare  state and not enforcing the Constitution  of the United States

